---
// Network faults (#417 (technique pages, part 1)): one seed with every
// network fault family on, then the same seed with the families you keep.
// The recorded run is src/runs/network.json; public/network.js runs the
// seed again in the engine.
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import Technique from "../../../layouts/Technique.astro";
import { url } from "../../../nav";
import { constNames, excerpt, repoFile } from "../../../lib/source.mjs";
import runs from "../../../runs/network.json";

const engine = repoFile("crates/moonpool-wasm-demo/src/engine.rs");
const families = constNames(engine, "NETWORK_FAULTS");
const chaos = excerpt(engine, "if chaos.network {", "}");
const mask = excerpt(engine, "let builder = match &config.network_faults {", "};");
---
<Technique
  title="Network faults"
  description="Cut a link, clog it, close a connection: the faults that TCP code sees."
  guide={[
    "part3-building/10-network-faults.md",
    "part3-building/07-chaos.md",
    "part3-building/28-swarm-testing.md",
    "appendix/04-fault-reference.md",
  ]}
>
  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>On a real network, a link stops for some seconds, a connection closes in the middle of a request, a peer
    accepts bytes and never answers. Each of these is rare on one machine and frequent on a thousand. A test
    on a laptop network almost never sees them, so the code that handles them is the code with no test.</p>

  <h2>The technique</h2>
  <p>moonpool simulates the network at the level of TCP connections, as FoundationDB does: the faults are
    the ones that application code sees. There are {families.length} families. Each family is one <code>NetworkFault</code> variant and one bit of the fault mask. A seed draws when each fault starts and
    how long it stays. With <code>ChaosMode::Swarm</code>, each seed also switches off a random set of
    families, because a lot of a few faults finds more bugs than a little of each.</p>
  <p>Each fault the simulator injects is an event of the run (<code>sim_fault</code>, with its kind). The
    workload does not know about it. It sees only late, lost or refused messages.</p>

  <h2>The live run</h2>
  <p>This seed has every network family on: two partitions, clogs and a black hole. Switch some families
    off, then run the same seed again. The table counts the faults of each kind in the two runs. With other
    families, the same seed makes other choices, so it is another run. It is still one run per seed and per
    set of families: run it again and you get the same events.</p>
  <div class="breakout">
    <section class="run" id="run" aria-label="A live run"></section>
    <form class="run run-under" id="families">
      <div class="run-head label"><span>network fault families of this run</span></div>
      <div class="switches">
        {families.map((f) => (
          <label><input type="checkbox" name="family" value={f} checked /> {f}</label>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="switches">
        <button class="btn small" type="submit">Run the same seed</button>
        <button class="btn small" type="button" id="families-all">All on</button>
        <button class="btn small" type="button" id="families-none">All off</button>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table class="run-table" id="fault-table"></table></div>
      <p class="run-out" id="families-out" aria-live="polite"></p>
    </form>
    <noscript><p>The live run needs JavaScript.</p></noscript>
  </div>
  <script is:inline type="application/json" id="runs" set:html={JSON.stringify(runs).replace(/</g, "\\u003c")} />

  <h2>The code</h2>
  <p>The site's engine turns on network chaos with one entry of the builder:</p>
  <Code theme="css-variables" lang="rust" code={chaos} />
  <p>The switches above set a mask. A family that is not in the mask never fires, whatever the seed draws:</p>
  <Code theme="css-variables" lang="rust" code={mask} />
  <p>The server and the client of the register do not change. They use <code>ctx.network()</code>, and the
    simulated network decides what each connection does.</p>
  <script is:inline type="module" src={url("network.js")}></script>
</Technique>
